  {
   "slug": "ep-22-every-typeface-has-a-voice",
   "title": "Ep. 22: Every typeface has a voice",
   "date": "2026-10-06T06:00:00",
   "excerpt": "Why road signs, menus and wedding invitations all speak in different voices, with a type designer who draws letters for a living.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You hear a typeface before you read it. A wedding invitation whispers, a road sign speaks clearly, a sale poster shouts. Type designer Lena Varga has spent twenty years drawing letters, and she explains how those voices are made.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We talk about the letters that are hardest to draw (the lowercase g, every time), why airports love certain shapes, and how she tested a new font by printing it on a cake.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Chapters</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"pw-timestamps\"} --><ul class=\"wp-block-list pw-timestamps\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li><code>00:00</code> Reading a menu out loud</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><code>08:10</code> The hardest letter</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><code>21:45</code> Signs you can read at 100 km/h</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><code>33:00</code> The cake test</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --><!-- wp:pullquote --><figure class=\"wp-block-pullquote\"><blockquote><p>A good typeface is like a good narrator: you stop noticing it, and the story gets louder.</p></blockquote></figure><!-- /wp:pullquote --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Lena's new typeface is free for schools. She asked us to say so, and we were happy to.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "season-2"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "design"
   ],
   "featured_media": "ep-type",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false
  },
  {
   "slug": "ep-21-the-people-who-name-colours",
   "title": "Ep. 21: The people who name colours",
   "date": "2026-09-29T06:00:00",
   "excerpt": "Somebody has to decide that a grey is called 'Harbour Fog'. We met the team that does it for a paint company.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Somewhere, someone is being paid to decide whether a pale green is 'Pea Shoot' or 'Garden Gate'. We spent a morning with the small team that names colours for a paint manufacturer, and it turns out to be harder, and stranger, than it sounds.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>They test names on strangers, avoid anything that sounds edible in a bathroom, and keep a list of rejected names that is better than most poetry.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>The name has to work in a shop, a catalogue and a phone call.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>It must not sound like another colour.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>It should make you imagine a room, not a paint tin.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We also hear about the colour that took eighteen months to name, and why the team keeps a jar of sea glass on the meeting table for inspiration.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Chapters</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"pw-timestamps\"} --><ul class=\"wp-block-list pw-timestamps\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li><code>00:00</code> Harbour Fog, explained</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><code>09:30</code> The rejected list</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><code>22:15</code> Why there are 400 whites</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><code>30:40</code> Name our studio's wall</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->",
   "categories": [
    "season-2"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "design",
    "language"
   ],
   "featured_media": "ep-colours",
