  {
   "slug": "behind-the-mic-how-we-edit-an-episode",
   "title": "Behind the mic: how we edit an episode",
   "date": "2026-09-08T08:00:00",
   "excerpt": "From a two-hour recording to a forty-minute episode: our editing process, our gear and the cuts that hurt the most.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A typical interview runs about two hours. The episode you hear is usually forty minutes. People often ask what happens in between, so here is our process, step by step.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Our editing steps</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} --><ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Listen once, without touching anything, and take notes.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Mark the three moments we would tell a friend about.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Build the story around those moments.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Cut repetition, keep the pauses that mean something.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Mix, level and listen again on cheap earphones.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ol><!-- /wp:list --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Editing is also where we fix our own mistakes. If a question came out tangled, we sometimes re-record it afterwards, and we always tell guests when we do.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What we record with</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:table --><figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Gear</th><th>What we use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Microphones</td><td>Two dynamic mics on desk arms</td></tr><tr><td>Interface</td><td>A small four-input recorder</td></tr><tr><td>Room</td><td>A spare room, two rugs and a lot of cushions</td></tr><tr><td>Editing</td><td>Any multitrack editor works; we use whatever is open</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><!-- /wp:table --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The best advice we ever got: the listener cannot see you nodding. Say 'yes' out loud.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "behind-the-mic"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "production"
   ],
   "featured_media": "ep-edit",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false
  },
  {
   "slug": "ep-19-night-trains-are-back",
   "title": "Ep. 19: Night trains are back",
   "date": "2026-09-01T06:00:00",
   "excerpt": "Falling asleep in one country and waking up in another: we take an overnight train and record the whole journey.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We booked two bunks on an overnight train and pressed record. This episode follows the journey from the departure board to breakfast in a different country, with a conductor, a chef and a very chatty family of five along the way.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Night trains nearly disappeared, and now they are coming back. We ask why, and whether a train can ever be as cheap as a flight.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Chapters</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"pw-timestamps\"} --><ul class=\"wp-block-list pw-timestamps\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li><code>00:00</code> Departure board</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><code>07:40</code> Meet the conductor</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><code>18:25</code> Dinner in the dining car</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><code>29:10</code> The 3am border</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><code>37:00</code> Breakfast somewhere new</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --><!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You don't sleep well on a train. You sleep happily, which is better.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>The conductor</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->",
   "categories": [
    "season-1"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "travel",
    "cities"
   ],
   "featured_media": "ep-train",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false
  }
